Create a Folder Structure for Your Simulation Model
Before you start modeling, we recommend to think about the structure in the Class Library of your
simulation model, as a clear structure will facilitate locating the objects.
To get an idea about structuring, you might want to take a look at the libraries that were installed, when
you set up Plant Simulation. By default these are located in C:\Program Files\Siemens\Tecnomatix Plant
Simulation XX\Libraries.
When you start modeling, we recommend to create new folders for the objects you use in your model,
rather than store the objects within the built-in folders. Otherwise the risk of changing the built-in objects
Create a Folder Structure for Your Simulation Model
Plant Simulation Help 10-79
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

inadvertently might have confusing and unpredictable effects. This also facilitates saving your library as an
object file, which you can then load into any existing simulation model.
We intended the folder UserObjects for this purpose, You can then add these objects to the tab User
Objects in the Toolbox.
You can also create subfolders and thus structure your Class Library clearly. Proceed as described below.
If you do not want to use the folder User Objects, you can create your own folder:
• Right-click Basis in the Class Library if you want to insert the new folder on the same level as the built-in
folders.
• Right-click any folder in the Class Library if you want to insert the new folder into that folder.
• Select New > Folder.
• Rename the folder: Click NewFolder once and type in a meaningful name. In our library we use
ApplicationObjects.
We also recommend to create a subfolder for the basic, built-in objects that your simulation model
uses, within the folder ApplicationObjects. In our library we named this subfolder BasicObjects. This
makes your library independent of the language of the model that the user selects and of the naming
Create a Folder Structure for Your Simulation Model
10-80 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

conventions of the built-in objects you use in your model. In addition, this simplifies loading the library into
other models, as this mechanism makes merging objects superfluous.
Compare Using Inheritance
Compare Save a Folder or an Object and Load it into Another Model
